  "live_records": {
    "q-70957": {
      "dom_id": "step-q70957",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=2t+2, \\\\ z=t+6\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$",
      "worked_solution": "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
